l to map.\n\nActually simpler: read_nametable for the top rows to see tile IDs and their positions; then read_tile to identify icons/numbers. But identifying digit tile ids: I can read tiles.\n\nAlternatively, just do differential search for each distinct value, and determine which counter it is by writing to it and observing which of the three numbers changes (position tells icon/type). Then to know rupee vs key vs bomb, use the icon: read the tile left of the number and read_tile to see its shape.\n\nLet me plan the differential search:\n- Rupees: I can gain rupees by killing enemies? On the start screen there might be no enemies. There's a cave at top-left (the sword cave). Entering it... Actually rupees can be obtained by cutting bushes? Let's see. The start screen has a cave. Hmm.\n\nBetter approach: all three counts are nonzero and distinct (11, 8, 7). Use unchanged diffs across play, then equals filters.\n\nBut \"unchanged\" over 2048 bytes with walking will leave many survivors (the whole screen area, most of RAM). Then iterate: diff equals 11 with from, etc. Actually the skill's method: snapshot twice across varied play, diff unchanged, then equals 11, then narrow with more unchanged rounds. Then bisect remaining with writes.\n\nBut there's a much stronger approach: write a candidate value and observe HUD. But to bisect we need the number to redraw. Does the HUD redraw only on change? In Zelda, the HUD updates when the value changes (the routine draws it). Writing RAM then the game redraws when value changes? Actually the HUD is drawn when the rupee count changes via the item-get routine. Writing RAM directly may not trigger redraw. However, in Zelda the HUD updates every frame? I think the HUD text is updated by a routine each frame or when a flag is set. Let me test: write to a candidate and step frames / screenshot.\n\nAlternative safer approach for proof: find addresses, then trigger a redraw by a game event (e.g., collect a rupee or enter a cave). Hmm.\n\nLet me think about known Zelda 1 RAM map from memory (but I must not rely on reference; the user says without any reference, use differential search). Still, my own judgement can guide. I shouldn't look up. Let me do it properly.\n\nApproach:\n1. snapshot \"hud0\" at current overworld state.\n2. Walk around (e.g., right then left) = varied play; snapshot \"hud1\".\n3. diff unchanged from hud0->hud1.\n4. Then diff equals 11 (from that), equals 8, equals 7 in separate named diffs. Actually diff with `from` chains: each narrows. Let me do:\n   - d_un = unchanged (hud0,hud1) -> survivors\n   - d11 = equals 11 from d_un -> the rupee byte among others.\n   Hmm but hearts = 3, and many bytes equal 11.\n\nActually better: use the fact that between two snapshots where nothing at all changes except position/time, the counters remain 11,8,7. Combine equals filters: a byte that equals 11 in snap1 and remained 11 across multiple snapshots. That's still many.\n\nThe real narrowing: make the values change by playing! We can collect rupees, bombs, keys. On the first overworld screen, are there enemies? The start screen (row 7 col 7) has no enemies typically but has a cave. Rupees can be obtained... hmm.\n\nActually the file has 11 rupees, 8 bombs? 7 keys? That's a lot already (probably a hacked/test save from previous session). We could spend them:\n- Bombs: use a bomb (press B if bomb is equipped) → bomb count decreases. But B item is currently a sword? The B box shows a sword icon? Actually the B box shows a small item. Hmm.\n- Keys: use a key by opening a door in a dungeon — hard.\n- Rupees: spend at a shop — hard. Or gain by killing enemies.\n\nAlternatively, we can change counts by picking up items in the world. On the starting screen there might be a cave with rupees? Hmm.\n\nSimplest reliable: use the \"make the display redraw on demand\" method with bisect. In Zelda, the HUD rupee/key/bomb counts are redrawn whenever the value changes (the game has a routine that updates the HUD on count change). If I write RAM directly, does the HUD redraw? Possibly not until next HUD update. But in Zelda 1, the HUD is drawn during the \"update HUD\" routine that runs when the game is not scrolling, every frame? Actually I believe Zelda 1 updates the HUD digits each frame from RAM (there's a routine). Let me just test empirically: write a value to a random harmless RAM byte won't help. Instead, let me test on a known candidate.\n\nActually let's just start with differential search using play actions. Let me find a way to get rupees.\n\nOption: Kill enemies to get rupees. Walk right/east to next screens which have Octoroks. Kill with sword → drops rupees randomly. That's slow but doable.\n\nBetter option: gain bombs/rupees from the cave? The start screen cave gives the sword (if not already).\n\nHmm, wait — actually the simplest: there are 11 rupees. We can LOSE rupees? Buying something costs rupees. On the starting screen there's a cave at top; inside is an old man who gives the sword. Not a shop.\n\nWalk to a shop? There's a shop in a cave on some screen. Too far.\n\nAlternative: Bombs are usable if the B item is a bomb. Looking at the B box, it shows a small icon; the A box shows a sword? Let me check. In the first screenshot the B box had a small blue crescent (sword?) and A box empty. In the overworld screenshot, B box shows a sword, A box empty. So B = sword. Can't spend bombs.\n\nHmm. But we could change the item? No.\n\nAlternative approach for changing counts: pick up a rupee. Rupee drops from enemies. Let's go east/north and kill enemies. Or there are rupee tiles under bushes/rocks.\n\nActually, maybe easier: the 3 hearts. To change hearts, take damage. Walking into an enemy reduces hearts. That's a great differential event! Enemies on adjacent screens. So hearts can be found by losing health.\n\nFor rupees/bombs/keys, we can use the \"unchanged + equals + bisect\" method.\n\nBut wait, there's another elegant approach: write_memory to change a value, and if the HUD redraws, we can identify it. In Zelda 1, I recall the HUD does update automatically. Let me just test with a known-ish candidate after narrowing.\n\nLet me structure this efficiently. I'll delegate? No, keep it in-session; it's one emulator.\n\nPlan of attack:\nA. Locate ","truncated":true}
