display \"A\" because its value is not 8. Let me determine the actual stored value by finding the address first, then reading the byte. That resolves everything.\n\nLet me now find the addresses by writing. But 24 candidates. Let me reduce using a trick: the counter bytes are likely read by the HUD-drawing routine; maybe they're copied to zero page. The equals-8 candidate 0x000A (=8) is zero page. The equals-7 candidate 0x006F (=7). Hmm, zero page 0x000A and 0x006F.\n\nActually! zero page 0x005B=11 was a copy of rupees. So zero-page copies exist. Maybe 0x000A=8 is the key count copy and 0x000B=13?? no. Hmm, we saw zero page: 0x0000=39,0x0001=33,0x0002=7,0x0003=36,0x0004=1,0x0005=1,0x0006=0,0x0007=255,0x0008=15,0x0009=6,0x000A=8,0x000B=13,0x000C=11,0x000D=3,0x000E=2,0x000F=38.\n\nHmm: 0x000A=8, 0x000B=13, 0x000C=11, 0x000D=3, 0x000E=2. Could be scratch.\n\nActually, maybe the middle HUD counter value is 8 and the byte is 0x066E? but writing 0x066E changed hearts. Hmm, unless writing 0x066E=55 changed the middle counter (to something) AND incidentally the heart display because 0x066E is also... no.\n\nHmm wait, when I wrote 0x066E=55 and 0x066F=66, the screenshot showed hearts multiplied and the middle showed maybe \"XA\". Actually maybe the middle didn't change at all (still 0x0A) and I just thought it did. And the hearts changed because 0x066E/0x066F are heart bytes.\n\nLet me test that: write 0x066E=55, read nametable row5/row6. And separately check hearts.\n\nOK let me be systematic and fast. I'll run a batch of experiments using the paused emulator + step_frame.\n\nProcedure per candidate: \n1. write candidate = 5\n2. step_frame 2 (or play 5)\n3. read_nametable row3-6, x11-14\n4. note change\n5. restore candidate's original value.\n\nTo minimize, I'll test the most likely ones first.\n\nMost likely key/bomb addresses: given rupee=0x066D, the neighbors 0x066E,0x066F. Let me test 0x066E and 0x066F explicitly by reading the HUD.\n\nLet me do: load state 2 (clean). Then write 0x066E=5, play 5, read nametable. Then write 0x066E back? state reload will restore.\n\nLet me do it for 0x066E and 0x066F.\n\nActually I realize the earlier test wrote both; let me isolate. Write 0x066E=5 alone.\n\nLet me also reconsider: perhaps the HUD counters are: rupee (0x066D), key (0x066E), bomb (0x066F) BUT the HUD display for keys/bombs","truncated":true}
